        └── RSA Authentication Manager
        │
        ▼
POLICY
Allow only the explicitly required flow
        │
        ▼
RESOURCE
```

So your shorthand is actually very good:

> **WHO:** Keycloak
> **WHAT:** LDAP/MFA authentication protocols
> **WHERE:** AD and RSA
> **WHY:** Authenticate/authorize a defined population for a defined service

And I would add **HOW**:

> **HOW:** explicitly permitted network flows enforced by firewall/NSX policy.

That last piece is important when explaining this to Security.

### We're not proposing removing segmentation

We're changing **what the segmentation represents**.

Today you have something approximately like:

```text
DOMAIN | FIREWALL | SEGMENT1
```

and membership/location in those networks carries a lot of implicit trust.

The greenfield model could become:

```text
                VCF PRIVATE CLOUD
                       │
                Security Policy
                       │
       ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
       │               │                │
    Identity        Service          Workload
    Services        Services          VPCs
       │               │                │
      AD             GitLab          Developer
      RSA            Jira            Workloads
                    Confluence
```

Then NSX/firewalls still enforce boundaries, but policies describe **required communication**, rather than merely reproducing the historical zones.

For example:

```text
SOURCE
Keycloak nodes

DESTINATION
tc.org Domain Controllers

PURPOSE
User authentication / lookup

ALLOWED
LDAPS 636

DENIED
Everything else
```

And separately:

```text
SOURCE
Keycloak nodes

DESTINATION
RSA Authentication Manager

PURPOSE
Privileged MFA

ALLOWED
Only RSA-required protocol(s)

DENIED
Everything else
```

That's actually **stronger segmentation in one sense**, because we're defining the permitted relationship between specific services instead of saying “Segment1 can communicate with Domain under these broad rules.”

