## DNS and NTP just became our first interesting bootstrap problem

This is where our cyber-recovery scenario diverges from an ordinary VCF installation.

VCF deployment planning requires the component FQDNs/IPs and management-domain network configuration; DNS and NTP are part of the management-domain configuration. Broadcom's planning process specifically has you populate management, deployment, and configuration inputs before handing those values to the Installer. ([VMware Blogs][1])

The practical bring-up guidance is stronger: required component FQDNs need to resolve before deployment, with forward and reverse DNS, and time synchronization needs to be available. ([Journal of Intelligent Infrastructure][3])

Our attack scenario says:

```text
Normal VCF deployment

Enterprise DNS ───────┐
Enterprise NTP ───────┼──► VCF Installer ──► VCF
Enterprise Repo ──────┘


OUR RECOVERY

Enterprise DNS ── X
Enterprise NTP ── X?
Enterprise Repo ─ X
AD              ─ X
GitLab          ─ X
RKE2            ─ X

       functioning ESXi
              ↑
═══════════ BOUNDARY ═══════════
              ↑
              ?
              ↑
        VCF Installer
              ↑
             VCF
```

Therefore, **DNS definitely has to be solved below VCF in our DAG**.

NTP also has to be solved below VCF, but it may be easier: if surviving physical routers/switches or some other infrastructure below our boundary can provide a trusted NTP source, we don't necessarily need to manufacture one inside the bootstrap VM.

That's a design question rather than a VMware requirement.

