## Test 1 — Learn and capture

The goal is **not automation**. It's to discover reality.

Start from our boundary, manually deploy the VCF Installer and use its normal wizard. Every time we have to answer a question, create something, fix DNS, change an IP, adjust an MTU, supply a password, download an artifact or resolve a precheck, it goes into the register.

Success is:

```text
Functional ESXi
      ↑
VCF Installer
      ↑
Manual configuration
      ↑
All prechecks pass
      ↑
VCF 9 builds
      ↑
VCF healthy
      ↑
Known-good JSON captured
```

Broadcom's planning guidance specifically recommends collecting the infrastructure prerequisites/configuration before transferring those inputs into VCF Installer. ([VMware Blogs][3])

Every new fact learned becomes:

**`DISCOVERED DURING BUILD`**

For example:

> `DNS-017 | Installer requires PTR for component X | DISCOVERED DURING BUILD | Test 1 | Failure XYZ disappeared after PTR creation`

That's how this becomes institutional engineering knowledge rather than someone's notebook.

## Test 2 — Reproduce

Destroy VCF #1.

Return to the boundary.

This time use the captured JSON rather than rebuilding the design from memory. Broadcom explicitly supports saving JSON for future builds/reference. ([VMware Blogs][4])

Success criterion:

> **Same starting boundary + known-good configuration → equivalent healthy VCF.**

This is where `CFG-003` can move:

```text
TO VERIFY IN LAB
        ↓
    CONFIRMED
```

And importantly, **Test 2 tells us whether our JSON really represents the desired state**.

