## This could actually improve our Test 1–4 methodology

Instead of fighting OPS for four blades right now, I'd deliberately make the staging VCF environment disposable:

```text
                 PHYSICAL ESXi 8 LAB
                         ↑
              Nested Lab Foundation
                         ↑
        ┌────────┬───────┼───────┬────────┐
        ↑        ↑       ↑       ↑        ↑
      ESX9-01  ESX9-02 ESX9-03 ESX9-04 Recovery VM
        │        │       │       │
        └────────┴───┬───┴───────┘
                     ↑
                Nested vSAN
                     ↑
                VCF Installer
                     ↑
                  VCF 9
```

Now think about what that does to our CI/CD idea.

The pipeline could potentially destroy and recreate the **entire nested environment**.

```text
New VCF candidate
       ↑
Build nested ESX 9 hosts
       ↑
Build nested vSAN
       ↑
Start Recovery VM
       ↑
Start DNS + Nginx depot
       ↑
Deploy VCF Installer
       ↑
Submit approved JSON
       ↑
Deploy VCF
       ↑
Validate
       ↑
Generate report
       ↑
DESTROY ENTIRE NESTED LAB
```

Then do it again.

That is actually a **better automated recovery test** than using four persistent physical lab hosts, because every run can begin from an almost completely known state.

There is one caveat: it proves the **VCF configuration and recovery process**, but it does not completely prove the production physical hardware path. Before production release I'd eventually want a smaller number of milestone builds performed against actual Dell hardware.

So I'd distinguish two validation levels:

**`LAB-VALIDATED`** — complete clean reconstruction succeeded on nested ESX/vSAN.

**`HARDWARE-VALIDATED`** — reconstruction succeeded against the actual Dell/ESX/vSAN configuration intended for production.

That distinction should go into our provenance manifest and verification report.

And there's a nice consequence for our DAG: **the nested lab infrastructure belongs below the staging boundary, not inside the production VCF recovery DAG.** The production DAG stays exactly as we designed it; staging simply provides a virtual implementation of the same foundation contract.

I think this is probably the direction I'd pursue before asking OPS to surrender four production-capable blades. It lets us start learning VCF 9, build the Recovery VM, develop the JSON, exercise nested vSAN, and hammer Tests 1–4 repeatedly **without touching their capacity at all**.

