# 3. Binaries/artifacts we need

There's an important distinction in your question:

### vSAN doesn't have a separate "vSAN ISO"

vSAN is integrated into ESX/vSphere. We're not going to download:

```text
ESX.iso
vSAN.iso
```

and install them separately.

The main artifact categories for our lab are approximately:

| Artifact                                |                                     Need | Purpose                             |
| --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------: | ----------------------------------- |
| **VCF Installer appliance OVA**         |                                  **Yes** | Day-0 VCF deployment engine         |
| **ESX 9 installer ISO**                 |                                  **Yes** | Install nested ESX hosts            |
| **ESX 9 offline depot/bundle ZIP**      | **Yes for repository/lifecycle testing** | Depot/vLCM/VCF lifecycle            |
| **vCenter Server appliance ISO/bundle** |                 **Yes in VCF depot/BOM** | VCF deploys management vCenter      |
| **NSX appliance/bundle**                |                 **Yes in VCF depot/BOM** | VCF deploys management-domain NSX   |
| **VCF Operations bundle/appliance**     |                                  **Yes** | VCF operations/fleet management     |
| **VCF Automation bundle/appliance**     |               **Yes for full VCF stack** | Automation/self-service component   |
| **SDDC Manager bundle/appliance**       |                       **Yes in VCF BOM** | VCF instance component              |
| **vSAN separate ISO**                   |                                   **No** | vSAN is integrated with vSphere/ESX |
| **VCF Download Tool**                   |                                  **Yes** | Populate/manage offline depot       |
| **VCF metadata/catalog files**          |                                  **Yes** | Depot/BOM/component discovery       |
| **Deployment JSON**                     |                      **Generated by us** | Desired VCF configuration           |

Broadcom says the VCF Installer deploys the stack including vCenter, NSX, VCF Operations and VCF Automation; SDDC Manager is also installed as a VCF 9 component. ([VMware Blogs][1])

**I would not manually hunt down every appliance OVA individually if the VCF Download Tool can populate the appropriate release into our depot.**

That's actually one of the first things we should learn in Test #1.

Broadcom's offline-depot procedure says to download the **VCF Download Tool** from Broadcom Support Portal → VMware Cloud Foundation → Drivers & Tools. The package includes the VCF Download Tool and bundle-transfer utility. 

The depot also isn't merely a directory full of ISOs. VCF 9 uses metadata including `productVersionCatalog.json` and `vcfManifest.json` to recognize binaries, compatibility and HCL information. 

That reinforces our Nginx design:

```text
Recovery VM
│
└── Nginx :443
     │
     └── /vcf-depot/
          │
          ├── metadata/
          │    ├── productVersionCatalog.json
          │    └── vcfManifest.json
          │
          ├── ESX
          ├── vCenter
          ├── NSX
          ├── Operations
          ├── Automation
          └── other BOM artifacts
```

And Broadcom requires the offline web server to support TLS 1.2/1.3; their guidance calls for substantial storage, potentially up to/about 1 TB depending on downloaded bundles. 

