Yes. **VCF 9.1.1 is a sensible fixed baseline for this project**, especially given that we're expecting months of lab work rather than trying to chase releases during Tests 1–4.

One date correction before we lock it into the DAG: Broadcom's current release table lists **VCF 9.1.1 GA as September 3, 2026**, not August 26. Broadcom's announcement is also dated September 3. ([VMware Blogs][1])

More importantly, starting with VCF 9, Broadcom moved the VCF components onto a common versioning/release convention, so using **9.1.1 as our baseline** gives us a cleaner BOM concept than older VCF releases.  NSX 9.1.1, for example, is also listed as released September 3, build 25691509. 

I'd freeze the lab definition as:

```text
VCF LAB BASELINE 001

Platform Baseline:     VMware Cloud Foundation 9.1.1
Release Date:           03 September 2026
Validation Model:       Nested ESX 9.1.1 / Nested vSAN
Outer Hypervisor:       Existing ESXi 8 lab cluster

Purpose:
  Architecture validation
  Day-0 reconstruction validation
  Offline repository validation
  IaC/recovery validation
  NOC/operator validation
  Failure-injection validation

NOT:
  Production hardware validation
```

And this actually makes our repository/provenance design even more important. We should **not update this lab baseline every time Broadcom releases 9.1.1.x/9.1.2/9.2 or a security patch while we're developing the initial process**.

Instead:

```text
VCF 9.1.1
    │
    ▼
BASELINE-001
    │
    ├── Test 1
    ├── Test 2
    ├── Test 3
    ├── Test 4
    ├── Repeat Recovery
    ├── NOC Test
    └── Failure Injection
             │
             ▼
      ARCHITECTURE PROVEN
```

A newer Broadcom release becomes a **candidate**, not an automatic replacement:

```text
VCF 9.1.2 / future update
          │
          ▼
      CANDIDATE-002
          │
          ▼
automated nested reconstruction
          │
          ▼
 automated validation
          │
          ▼
 engineering review
          │
          ▼
 approved new baseline
```

That's exactly the lifecycle we've been designing.

One thing we should account for when I produce the **exact 9.1.1 build guide**: VCF 9.1 introduced mandatory **VCF Management Services**, and Broadcom says that layer requires a minimum of **12 IP addresses**, with another 18 potentially added for additional scenarios.  That's the kind of 9.1-specific requirement that needs to get captured before we lay out our nested networks/IP plan.

So from here forward, I'll treat **VCF 9.1.1 as `LAB-BASELINE-001`** unless we explicitly decide to change it.

The next useful deliverable is therefore no longer a generic VCF 9 walkthrough. It should be a **VCF 9.1.1-specific Nested Lab Build Book**: exact Broadcom 9.1.1 artifacts/BOM, four nested ESX VM specifications, virtual disks for vSAN, nested networking/port groups, IP/FQDN worksheet, DNS records, VCF Management Services addresses, Recovery VM, Nginx depot layout, VCF Installer, and then the exact Test 0 → Test 1 execution sequence. That would give you something you can actually sit beside the keyboard and work through when the downloads are ready.

