Built it. I turned what we've established into a working **VCF 9.1.1 Nested Lab Build Book v0.1** rather than just another conceptual outline.

[Download the VCF 9.1.1 Nested Lab Build Book v0.1](sandbox:/mnt/data/VCF_9.1.1_Nested_Lab_Build_Book_v0.1.docx)

It's an 11-page build document covering the isolated sandbox architecture, proposed deterministic network/IP plan, four nested ESX 9.1.1 hosts, nested vSAN approach, Recovery/IaC VM, bootstrap DNS, Nginx HTTPS depot, VCF Installer, artifact/download manifest, Test 0 through Test 4+, failure injection, provenance, Gate A, and the mandatory physical-hardware validation before Gate B/production.

I also made the **OPS download checklist** explicit. One useful 9.1-specific finding while researching this: the VCF Download Tool's default `INSTALL` query does **not** necessarily return every component, and ESX installation media is deliberately handled separately because VCFDT isn't the bare-metal ESX installer. Broadcom documents explicit component queries for things such as `VCENTER`, `NSX_T_MANAGER`, `SDDC_MANAGER_VCF`, `VROPS`, `VRA`, `VSP`, etc. 

Also captured in the book: VCF 9.1 depot authentication uses a **Software Depot ID + Activation Code**, replacing the older download-token model.  The offline depot needs the metadata/catalog/HCL information in addition to binaries and should be served over TLS 1.2/1.3; Broadcom recommends planning substantial depot storage, potentially up to 1 TB depending on what we retain. 

And I reserved **30 contiguous IPs** inside the proposed management subnet for VCF Management Services. Broadcom's minimum is 12, but it permits up to 30 and identifies the additional addresses for future components/scale-out. Since you have free range in the sandbox, there's little reason for us to paint ourselves into a corner with 12. 

The nested approach itself remains well aligned with what we're trying to prove: Broadcom has documented its own engineers using nested ESX VMs with virtual disks for vSAN when physical hardware wasn't available, while a new VCF instance still has the four-host management-cluster requirement. ([VMware Blogs][1])

Once OPS gets you the download path, **I would not jump straight into installing anything**. Bring me the filenames/version/build numbers they can see for 9.1.1. We can reconcile that actual download inventory against the build book first and create **Artifact Manifest 001**. That gives us the first real input to the provenance/recovery system rather than discovering halfway through Test 1 that we're missing a component.

